WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.771 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Hi. Marques.
- Hey, I'm Ryan.
  Nice to meet you.

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.139 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Good to meet you, Ryan.
- Thank you.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.673 align:start size:50% position:50%
Good to see you.
So in this box

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:11.411
is a milestone piece of tech.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:13.213 align:start position:0%
I just want to have you open it
and give me your thoughts.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.383 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Ooh.
- ( laughs )

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:19.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I love these.
- It's so cool-looking.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:21.154 align:start size:56% position:44%
This is definitely
older than me.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.555 align:start size:59% position:41%
I've never actually
seen one of these.

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:24.791 size:84% position:42%
You want to give it a spin?

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:27.493
This is functioning?

00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:30.797
<i> ( synth rock playing )</i>

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:41.641
That's extremely cool.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:49.315
<i> I'm Marques Brownlee
 and I review dope new tech.</i>

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:52.351
<i> But on this show,
 I'm rewinding the clock</i>

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:54.454
<i> to discover
 the tech of the past</i>

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.657
<i> that changed
 our lives forever.</i>

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.694
<i> This is
 "Retro Tech: Walkman."</i>

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.197
Hey, what's up, guys?
MKBHD here.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.101
So, spoiler alert,
I've never used a Walkman.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.502
I'm very unfamiliar with it.

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:10.536
and this will be literally

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.839
my first time ever seeing it.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.374
The first ever
portable music player,

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:20.379
Sony introduced
the TPS-L2 Walkman in 1979

00:01:20.412 --> 00:01:22.248
and changed
the music game forever.

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:23.549
I wasn't alive back then.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:25.885
In fact,
half of people alive now

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:28.888
were not alive when
the Walkman was introduced.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:30.857
Also, this pink text
is not an accident.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.558
It's everywhere.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:34.894
So let's go ahead
and get one out the box.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:37.263
Paperwork-- this has never
gone out of style.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:39.866
Whoa.
These are the headphones.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:42.235
Flimsy. Very lightweight.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.205
That just leaves
the Walkman itself.

00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:48.374
In a leather case--

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:51.344
this is like
a little pocketbook.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.412
Oh, my gosh.

00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:55.181
It's pretty chunky.

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:57.383
AA batteries.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.619
Tactile buttons.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.588
So that's your fast-forward,
your rewind.

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:04.524
Play/pause. Stop.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:08.694
Immensely satisfying.
You don't get that today.

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:11.430
So there's only
one more thing to do,

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:14.367
and that is
listen to some music.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.269
Beastie Boys.

00:02:22.641 --> 00:02:24.511
Oh, I have to struggle
with everything, don't I?

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:28.214
Nope.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.886
I just want to play my tape.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:37.957
Ah.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.293
This little bracket down here?

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.562
Go ahead and slide it in
on that bracket.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:49.602
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.306
Whew.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:54.840
Wow. These actually
don't sound bad.

00:02:54.873 --> 00:02:56.809
<i> ( music continues )</i>

00:02:56.842 --> 00:03:00.546
<i> This year is the 40th
 anniversary of the Walkman,</i>

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.315
<i> and I want to know
 how this piece of tech</i>

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.351
<i> jumpstarted portable music
 as we know it today.</i>

00:03:05.384 --> 00:03:07.420
<i> ( music stops )</i>

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.822 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Hey. Marques.
- Hey, Marques. Americus.

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:11.424 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Good to meet you.
- Nice to meet you, too.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.859 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
- Wow.
- All right.

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:16.262 align:start size:69% position:31%
So to start off,
I'm going to have you
check under your seat.

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:17.396 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Like, right now?
- Go ahead.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:18.931
- Oh, no.
- Aah!

00:03:18.964 --> 00:03:20.333
This is pretty cool.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:21.901
Wow.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.369
This brings back memories.

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:25.438 line:0%
This guy!

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.840 line:0%
I have not seen
one of these things

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:29.709 line:0%
in I don't even know
how long.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.844 line:0%
Fits perfect, yeah.

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.913 line:0%
Wow. This is pretty dope, man.

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:37.850 align:start size:50% position:50%
Can you take me
back to the '70s

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:40.353 align:start size:66% position:34%
and what the world
of the audio was like

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.687 size:84% position:58%
before the Walkman existed?

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.522
Yeah,
the world of audio was fun

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:45.524
because there was
a sort of preference

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:46.859
for stuff getting bigger.

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:48.828
You know,
you wanted music to be loud,

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:49.829
you wanted it to be booming.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:50.997
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.031
<i> ♪ Everybody ♪</i>

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.032
Seth:
Before Walkmen,

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:55.935
music was innately social,

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.703
blaring into
the communal airwaves.

00:03:57.736 --> 00:03:59.505
People carried
around boom boxes.

00:03:59.538 --> 00:04:01.540
Boom boxes were the thing.

00:04:01.573 --> 00:04:03.276
The bigger it was,
the cooler you were.

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:05.044
Man on TV:
The radio that's about as big

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.545
as the Empire State Building

00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:08.547
and as loud as World War II.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.716
They were big, and they were
cumbersome to carry around.

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:12.885
It definitely
was not the type of device

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:15.721
that would fit in your pocket,
or even in a backpack.

00:04:15.754 --> 00:04:17.723
So the heads of Sony
had an idea,

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:19.725
and the idea was let's see

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:22.028
if we can make things
a bit more mobile,

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:24.297
so suddenly smaller
becomes better.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.898
Lisa:
Portable cassette players

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:27.933
definitely existed
before the Walkman,

00:04:27.966 --> 00:04:29.735
but they were very different.

00:04:29.768 --> 00:04:32.405
Some of them
were the size of VCRs

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:35.041
and they had these big
chunky buttons.

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.976
But they weren't
for listening to music.

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:39.045
They were for journalists
and secretaries.

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:41.847
So the heads of Sony
had an idea.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.350
Can you walk me through
the origin story of the Walkman?

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:46.919 line:0%
Seth:
So the founder of Sony

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:48.654 line:0%
was a huge audiophile.

00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:49.922
He loved opera music.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:53.459
And he's taking
this old cassette player

00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:56.429
that's designed
for recording notes
as a journalist might

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:59.699
and using that on the road
to listen to his opera music.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.934
<i> ( opera music playing )</i>

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:03.502
And he finds
this thing clunky.

00:05:03.535 --> 00:05:05.404
It's totally impractical.

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:06.906
There has to be a better way.

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:09.942
Lisa:
Masaru Ibuka tasked his team

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:11.711
to come up with a solution.

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:14.080
Seth: So they start
stripping away components.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.614
They start stripping
away parts.

00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:18.017
All the gears and gadgetry
and equipment

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.952
necessary to record
onto a tape?

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:21.620 line:0%
They get rid of that.

00:05:21.653 --> 00:05:24.457 line:8%
And what they're left with
is a small,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.459 line:0%
sleek, portable device

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.461 line:0%
that does basically one thing,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.796 line:0%
but does that one thing
really well.

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:33.966 line:0%
It lets you listen to music.

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:36.102
Lisa: And in 1979,
the Walkman was born.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:38.438
<i> ( opera music ends )</i>

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:42.975
Marques:
I wanted to learn even more

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.443
about the origin
of the Walkman,

00:05:44.476 --> 00:05:46.112 line:0%
so I got in touch
with Toshio Asai,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.481 line:0%
who was an engineer on
the original development team

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.851 line:0%
for the Walkman
at Sony in Tokyo.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.821 line:0%
( speaking Japanese )

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:14.974 line:0%
( speaking Japanese )

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:48.474
Marques:
The engineers at Sony
did such a good job

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:50.676
building the Walkman
that consumers would keep them

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:52.812
even if they broke.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:56.015
Nowadays, if our tech breaks,
we bring it in to be fixed.

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:58.951
But back then, everyone was
apparently pretty handy.

00:06:58.984 --> 00:07:00.719
So I've invited
my friend Judner,

00:07:00.752 --> 00:07:03.722
AKA "UrAvgConsumer,"
to help me out.

00:07:03.755 --> 00:07:05.891 line:0%
Between the two of us,
we have more than enough knowhow

00:07:05.924 --> 00:07:08.060 line:0%
to get into this tech.

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:10.696
- Thanks for joining me today.
- Thanks for having me, man.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:13.766 align:start position:0%
We're both YouTube creators.
We're both tech heads.

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:16.735 align:start size:66% position:34%
We're both born after
the Walkman came out.

00:07:16.768 --> 00:07:18.838 align:start size:75% position:25%
- This is heavy.
- Yeah.

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.706 align:start size:63% position:38%
So, what do you know
about the Walkman?

00:07:20.739 --> 00:07:22.575 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I know they use tapes.
- Mm-hmm.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:26.212 align:start size:63% position:38%
But outside of that?
Not too much.

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.581 align:start position:0%
Okay. So, we review
a lot of tech.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.583 align:start position:0%
When's the last time
you actually had to go in

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.052
and replace or fix something?

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.222 align:start size:81% position:19%
I don't think I ever have.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:37.623 size:78% position:39%
I can't think of any time

00:07:37.656 --> 00:07:39.625 align:start position:0%
I have actually
had to personally do it.

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:42.528 size:88% position:44%
So, one of the most common
problems with these Walkmen,

00:07:42.561 --> 00:07:45.764 align:start position:0%
the bands inside
that turn the tapes
and make them move...

00:07:45.797 --> 00:07:46.932
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:48.934 align:start position:0%
...would sometimes
stretch or break.

00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:50.035
<i> ( music stops )</i>

00:07:50.068 --> 00:07:51.670 align:start position:0%
So, with the right
instructions,

00:07:51.703 --> 00:07:55.641 size:81% position:41%
can these Walkmen be fixed
by your average consumer?

00:07:55.674 --> 00:07:58.010 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Sounds good.
- I guess we'll figure
  that out pretty soon.

00:07:58.043 --> 00:08:00.846 align:start position:0%
All right,
let's jump right in.

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:04.083 line:0%
Step one, remove
the first set of screws
from the side panel.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:08.554
- That's simple enough.
- Keep these screws safe.

00:08:08.587 --> 00:08:11.624 align:start position:0%
All right,
I've got doors coming off,

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:13.225 align:start position:0%
Oh!

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:15.961 align:start size:50% position:50%
Look at the guts
of that thing.

00:08:15.994 --> 00:08:18.931 size:78% position:39%
If I had to defuse a bomb
and it looked like this?

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:21.233 align:start size:63% position:38%
We're all gonna die.

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:23.969 align:start position:0%
There are 20 gears in here
and a motor.

00:08:24.002 --> 00:08:26.972 align:start position:0%
That's something
you don't see in today's tech.

00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:29.542
- Oh, there we go.
- That's your band.

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:31.143 size:84% position:42%
This is completely snapped.

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:33.913 size:78% position:39%
As far as my appreciation
for old tech right now?

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.814 align:start position:0%
I'm not sure
if this is making me

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:40.653 size:84% position:42%
appreciate it more or less.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:41.987 align:start size:69% position:31%
I think I'd appreciate

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.989 align:start size:78% position:22%
the person
who could do this for me.

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:45.891 align:start size:88% position:13%
True.

00:08:45.924 --> 00:08:47.560 align:start position:0%
This brings me
back to high school.

00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:50.062 size:78% position:39% line:0%
There's the second screw.

00:08:50.095 --> 00:08:51.997 align:start size:50% position:50% line:0%
Whew. There goes
the whole plate.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.899 size:88% position:44% line:0%
I'm going to get it, though.

00:08:53.932 --> 00:08:55.901 align:start position:0% line:0%
Oh! There we go.

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:59.204 align:start position:0%
So this is that big band.
It's a little stretched out.

00:08:59.237 --> 00:09:01.674 align:start position:0%
( light clatter )

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:03.776 align:start size:81% position:19%
That sounded like a screw.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.311
Oh, no.

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:07.780 align:start position:0%
Anyone have a giant magnet?
I think I'm going to

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:09.582 align:start position:0%
have to finish this
without that one screw,

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm going to commandeer
some of this extra tape

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
- so that the bracket stays.
- Oh, okay, MacGyver.

00:09:14.886 --> 00:09:16.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You know who MacGyver is?
- Yep.

00:09:16.655 --> 00:09:18.023 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Do you really?
- Nope.

00:09:18.056 --> 00:09:19.858 align:start size:38% position:63%
Uh-huh.

00:09:19.891 --> 00:09:24.830 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, replace the big band
with this new big band.

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:27.199 align:start position:0%
This has got to go
right over the top here.

00:09:27.232 --> 00:09:29.268 align:start position:0%
Boom! First band done.

00:09:29.301 --> 00:09:31.103 align:start position:0%
Your board is
all over the place.

00:09:31.136 --> 00:09:33.606 align:start position:0%
Yeah, this is--
it's like I'm dissecting
a frog here.

00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:36.208 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Oh, man.
- I did not follow
  instructions.

00:09:39.244 --> 00:09:40.346 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Whoa.
- ( light clatter )

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:42.681 align:start size:38% position:63%
No!

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:43.983 align:start position:0%
Was that a screw
you just dropped?

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:52.758 align:start position:0%
This is a lot more steps
than I was imagining.

00:09:52.791 --> 00:09:54.994 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
Control panel back.

00:09:55.027 --> 00:09:56.895 line:0%
All right,
I finally reached the point

00:09:56.928 --> 00:10:01.200 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
where I can test to see
if I've installed
the bands correctly

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:03.168 align:start position:0%
and if that one screw
was optional.

00:10:03.201 --> 00:10:07.072 align:start position:0%
We'll see.
It's not completely
put back together,

00:10:07.105 --> 00:10:09.908 align:start position:0%
but if this works,

00:10:09.941 --> 00:10:13.012 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
it says a lot about
my progress so far,
I'm just saying.

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:17.350 align:start size:69% position:31%
Let me jump in on this
to verify.

00:10:19.951 --> 00:10:21.920 align:start position:0%
Okay, please work.
Please work.

00:10:21.953 --> 00:10:24.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Play.
- ( high-pitched whine )

00:10:24.222 --> 00:10:28.293
( whine continues )

00:10:28.326 --> 00:10:30.195 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
As you can see and hear,

00:10:30.228 --> 00:10:32.698 align:start position:0%
something about my band
isn't quite right.

00:10:32.731 --> 00:10:34.400 align:start size:75% position:25%
Something's--
my tape it bro--

00:10:34.433 --> 00:10:36.068
Oh, no.

00:10:36.101 --> 00:10:38.404 align:start position:0% line:0%
Oh, it's jammed.

00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:40.205 align:start position:0%
It is completely
jammed up in there.

00:10:40.238 --> 00:10:42.274 align:start size:63% position:38%
You definitely
destroyed that tape.

00:10:42.307 --> 00:10:45.377 align:start position:0%
Whoa. Aww, that is
a heartbreaker.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.913 align:start size:47% position:53%
( sighs )
I feel stumped.

00:10:47.946 --> 00:10:50.816 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
Dang. All right,
let's talk about what
we learned here today.

00:10:50.849 --> 00:10:52.951 align:start size:53% position:47%
So, this is hard.

00:10:52.984 --> 00:10:54.787 align:start size:81% position:19%
- This is difficult.
- Very.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.355 align:start size:66% position:34%
I guess I just gained
a lot of respect

00:10:56.388 --> 00:10:58.190 align:start size:91% position:9%
for people who actually
successfully pulled that off.

00:10:58.223 --> 00:11:01.460 align:start size:78% position:22%
Trying to figure this out
with no knowledge prior?

00:11:01.493 --> 00:11:04.296 align:start size:78% position:22%
It's impossible. I'm okay
being born in this era.

00:11:04.329 --> 00:11:07.966 size:75% position:38%
Thank you, digital tech.

00:11:07.999 --> 00:11:10.002
Marques:
As much appreciation
as I now have

00:11:10.035 --> 00:11:11.336
for the Walkman
after trying to fix one,

00:11:11.369 --> 00:11:13.205
not everyone
appreciated the Walkman

00:11:13.238 --> 00:11:15.340
when it first came out.

00:11:15.373 --> 00:11:17.376
Seth: People were up in arms
over the Walkman.

00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:19.111
They just simply
thought it was unsafe.

00:11:19.144 --> 00:11:21.146
They thought if people
weren't paying attention,

00:11:21.179 --> 00:11:22.815
they would get hit by cars.

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:24.316
There was a lot of backlash.

00:11:24.349 --> 00:11:26.285
This one of the first times
that we saw technology

00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:29.054
allow you to close off
the world.

00:11:29.087 --> 00:11:31.323
Man on TV:
Is it the Me Generation
gone wild?

00:11:31.356 --> 00:11:33.826
A height of anti-social
behavior?

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:35.994
There were all sorts
of anti-Walkman rules

00:11:36.027 --> 00:11:37.796
kind of popping up everywhere.

00:11:37.829 --> 00:11:40.833
The most prominent was in
Woodbridge, New Jersey, 1982.

00:11:40.866 --> 00:11:44.169
The town passes a law
that basically bans you

00:11:44.202 --> 00:11:46.371
from listening to Walkmen
while you're crossing streets

00:11:46.404 --> 00:11:47.773
and doing a bunch
of other things.

00:11:47.806 --> 00:11:49.341
We are trying
to stop a tragedy

00:11:49.374 --> 00:11:51.176
before it happens
in Woodbridge.

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:55.848
To this day, this Walkman ban
still stands in New Jersey.

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:57.783
Having a Walkman
wasn't immediately cool.

00:11:57.816 --> 00:11:59.351
This was a novel device.

00:11:59.384 --> 00:12:01.320
People would be staring at you
if you wore it.

00:12:01.353 --> 00:12:03.856
So what Sony wanted to do

00:12:03.889 --> 00:12:05.390
was to try
to turn those stares

00:12:05.423 --> 00:12:07.493
from bad stares
to good stares.

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:10.362 line:0%
So the ad campaigns,
the marketing campaign,

00:12:10.395 --> 00:12:12.064 line:0%
it was all about featuring fun,

00:12:12.097 --> 00:12:15.501 line:0%
hip, attractive-looking people
using Walkmen in public

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:18.203 line:0%
so that people would think it
was an acceptable thing to do.

00:12:18.236 --> 00:12:23.041 line:0%
Announcer:
The tiny stereo cassette player
with truly incredible sound.

00:12:23.074 --> 00:12:24.409 line:0%
Lisa: Sony's early ads

00:12:24.442 --> 00:12:27.045 line:0%
showed people dancing
out in the streets,

00:12:27.078 --> 00:12:28.547 line:0%
and the tagline said,

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:30.182
"There's a revolution
in the streets."

00:12:30.215 --> 00:12:31.517
Because it was
so new to people.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.152
People weren't
used to the idea

00:12:33.185 --> 00:12:34.453
of bringing
their music around.

00:12:34.486 --> 00:12:35.454
They probably
didn't even realize

00:12:35.487 --> 00:12:36.955
that they wanted to bring

00:12:36.988 --> 00:12:38.891
their music around
before the Walkman.

00:12:38.924 --> 00:12:41.393 align:start size:66% position:34%
It's like carrying
your stereo with you,
you know?

00:12:41.426 --> 00:12:43.395 align:start size:72% position:28%
On your-- on your head.

00:12:43.428 --> 00:12:46.064
Sony's idea was we're going to
put them in the hands

00:12:46.097 --> 00:12:48.267
of some of the top people
in the industry.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.235
Lisa:
You saw really prominent

00:12:50.268 --> 00:12:52.504
famous people wearing it.

00:12:52.537 --> 00:12:55.207
It was very similar to what
a lot of companies do today,

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.242 line:0%
except now it's called
influence marketing,

00:12:57.275 --> 00:12:59.545 line:0%
and it's mostly done
on Instagram.

00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:02.247 line:0%
And this worked.
Eventually, the Walkman

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.083
became something
you wanted to show off.

00:13:05.116 --> 00:13:07.519
But one of the big problems

00:13:07.552 --> 00:13:09.021
with any piece
of portable gear

00:13:09.054 --> 00:13:10.556
is that you're not social.

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:12.591
You're kind of isolated
from the world around you.

00:13:12.624 --> 00:13:14.560
So, what Sony did is they put

00:13:14.593 --> 00:13:16.228
his and hers
headphone jacks on it

00:13:16.261 --> 00:13:18.430
so that two people
could listen at once.

00:13:18.463 --> 00:13:21.066 line:0%
So, suddenly,
this solo activity

00:13:21.099 --> 00:13:22.901 line:0%
could be a couples' activity.

00:13:22.934 --> 00:13:24.102
You could listen
to a song with your friend

00:13:24.135 --> 00:13:26.104
and share it with them.

00:13:26.137 --> 00:13:28.173
The Walkman also a had
a hot line button

00:13:28.206 --> 00:13:30.142
that when you press it,

00:13:30.175 --> 00:13:32.277
allowed you to speak to someone
else through the device

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:35.147
and it lowered the music
a little bit, too.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:36.615 align:start position:0%
I left my Walkman
headphones at home,

00:13:36.648 --> 00:13:41.286 align:start position:0%
but I do have this pair
of 2018 headphones.

00:13:41.319 --> 00:13:43.288 align:start position:0%
We can do some hot line.
Let's go ahead and hit play.

00:13:43.321 --> 00:13:45.991 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Okay.
-<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:13:46.024 --> 00:13:47.860 align:start position:0%
My ears.

00:13:47.893 --> 00:13:50.095 align:start position:0%
Oh, the mic's up here.
Can you hear me?

00:13:50.128 --> 00:13:51.930
- Yeah! Yeah.
- Did it work?

00:13:51.963 --> 00:13:53.465 align:start size:84% position:16%
- This is weird.
- It lowered my volume
  and yours.

00:13:53.498 --> 00:13:54.967 align:start size:78% position:22%
That's kind of brilliant.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.635 align:start size:78% position:22%
♪ What's love got to do ♪

00:13:56.668 --> 00:13:58.570 align:start size:41% position:59%
♪ Got to do ♪

00:13:58.603 --> 00:14:00.606 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It doesn't totally mute it.
- Yeah.

00:14:00.639 --> 00:14:02.307 align:start size:81% position:19%
- It just lowers it.
- It lowers it.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.877 align:start size:75% position:25%
When headphones are new,
when Walkmen are new.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.478 align:start size:59% position:41%
people tend to talk
at a higher volume

00:14:06.511 --> 00:14:07.646 align:start size:97% position:3%
- when you have headphones on.
- Definitely.

00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:09.314 align:start size:63% position:38%
Before the Walkman,
stereo was, like,

00:14:09.347 --> 00:14:11.383 align:start size:91% position:9%
it was cool,
but it wasn't this headphone,

00:14:11.416 --> 00:14:12.985 align:start size:66% position:34%
immersive experience.

00:14:13.018 --> 00:14:14.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right.
- That's what the Walkman did.

00:14:14.185 --> 00:14:15.988 align:start position:0%
That's amazing.

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:18.557
Lisa: Although the Walkman
gets a lot of the credit,

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:20.492
the headphones
that Sony designed

00:14:20.525 --> 00:14:22.895
really played into
the Walkman's success.

00:14:22.928 --> 00:14:26.965
Before the Walkman came out,
headphones were big

00:14:26.998 --> 00:14:29.501
and they weren't really designed
to be carried around with you.

00:14:29.534 --> 00:14:32.004
The Sony Walkman
really changed that.

00:14:32.037 --> 00:14:34.506
With the small headphones
that the Walkman came with,

00:14:34.539 --> 00:14:36.642
they reduced
the weight by tenfold,

00:14:36.675 --> 00:14:39.945
making it much easier
to carry around
with you and wear.

00:14:39.978 --> 00:14:44.283
Alex: So, the Walkman
didn't only revolutionize
portable audio,

00:14:44.316 --> 00:14:46.251
it revolutionized
the headphone market.

00:14:46.284 --> 00:14:48.186
Marques:
I love quality audio

00:14:48.219 --> 00:14:49.655
and I take my headphones
with me everywhere,

00:14:49.688 --> 00:14:52.357
so I want to experience
how headphone technology

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.026
has evolved over the years.

00:14:54.059 --> 00:14:57.162
Before the Walkman,
headphones were big and clunky.

00:14:57.195 --> 00:15:00.032
So, Sony solved this problem
by making lightweight,

00:15:00.065 --> 00:15:01.934
breathable headphones

00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:05.003
that ushered in the age
of personal headphones
since then.

00:15:05.036 --> 00:15:06.972
So, we have some of the best
and worst of those with us,

00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:08.373
and we're going to
give them a spin.

00:15:08.406 --> 00:15:09.942
You can't hear exactly
what I'm hearing

00:15:09.975 --> 00:15:11.209
since they're headphones,

00:15:11.242 --> 00:15:12.311
but we'll give you
the same effect.

00:15:12.344 --> 00:15:14.646
So this is Dope Or Nope.

00:15:14.679 --> 00:15:16.381
Let's get right into it.

00:15:16.414 --> 00:15:19.551
We're going to take it back
to before the Walkman--

00:15:19.584 --> 00:15:25.123
the 1971 Professional
Electrostatic Model Float.

00:15:25.156 --> 00:15:26.625
These are huge.

00:15:26.658 --> 00:15:28.093
I don't know what
this hole is for.

00:15:28.126 --> 00:15:29.461
Like, a ponytail hole?
Who knows?

00:15:29.494 --> 00:15:31.229
These are the only ones
we could find

00:15:31.262 --> 00:15:32.998
from pre-Walkman days.

00:15:33.031 --> 00:15:38.070
Wow. They don't even
stay over my--

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:40.605
Um, strong nope.

00:15:40.638 --> 00:15:43.508
Big clunky headphones.

00:15:43.541 --> 00:15:46.011
Moving up to the early 2000s,

00:15:46.044 --> 00:15:47.612
and it's a neckband style.

00:15:47.645 --> 00:15:49.047
Right behind my neck
like that.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.449
Let's listen.

00:15:50.482 --> 00:15:55.420
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:15:55.453 --> 00:15:58.256
These are actually
a little bit more bass heavy.

00:15:58.289 --> 00:16:01.693
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:16:01.726 --> 00:16:04.262
You can now be that guy
walking down the street.

00:16:04.295 --> 00:16:06.465
But the lack of adjustability
kind of kills it,

00:16:06.498 --> 00:16:08.667
so, nope.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.269
Headphones in general
have branched out

00:16:11.302 --> 00:16:12.738
in a bunch of
different purposes.

00:16:12.771 --> 00:16:15.240
Welcome to 1985.

00:16:15.273 --> 00:16:18.610
We have Sony's AM/FM
radio headphones.

00:16:18.643 --> 00:16:20.145
Big antenna.

00:16:20.178 --> 00:16:21.613
I have at one point in my life

00:16:21.646 --> 00:16:23.448
seen someone wearing these,

00:16:23.481 --> 00:16:26.284
and it was a neighbor
mowing his lawn.

00:16:26.317 --> 00:16:28.053
So, a new era of portability,

00:16:28.086 --> 00:16:31.223
and dare I say,
truly wireless?

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:33.658
Dope.

00:16:33.691 --> 00:16:36.128
We have on-ear headphones

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:38.430
as a sort of expression
of personal style.

00:16:39.497 --> 00:16:43.769
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:16:43.802 --> 00:16:47.372
Oh, they sound horrible.

00:16:47.405 --> 00:16:50.142
Pretty sure this button
is going to light some stuff up.

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:52.144
There it is.

00:16:54.112 --> 00:16:55.480
There could be a unicorn fan

00:16:55.513 --> 00:16:57.616
that really wants
headphones like this,

00:16:57.649 --> 00:16:59.584
and for that person
these are probably dope.

00:16:59.617 --> 00:17:02.721
But for me,
the foam is not very soft.

00:17:02.754 --> 00:17:05.524
The sound,
as I mentioned, trash.

00:17:05.557 --> 00:17:10.328
Despite the glory
of the unicorn horn, nope.

00:17:10.361 --> 00:17:11.763
The way we hear our music

00:17:11.796 --> 00:17:13.665
was forever changed
by the Walkman headphones,

00:17:13.698 --> 00:17:17.169
but how did that impact
the physical medium

00:17:17.202 --> 00:17:19.204
that contained
the music itself?

00:17:19.237 --> 00:17:20.505
<i> ♪ Keep on rock,
 keep, keep on rock ♪</i>

00:17:20.538 --> 00:17:21.807
Lisa:
When the Walkman took off,

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:24.376
the cassette tape
also took off.

00:17:24.409 --> 00:17:27.479
And three years after
the Walkman was released
in the United States,

00:17:27.512 --> 00:17:31.716
cassette tapes sales outsold
vinyls for the first time.

00:17:31.749 --> 00:17:34.152
Alex: The introduction
of the blank cassette tape

00:17:34.185 --> 00:17:36.354
kind of changed everything,
whether you were just

00:17:36.387 --> 00:17:39.124
grabbing music from your
personal collection

00:17:39.157 --> 00:17:40.525
or you were stealing it
from the radio.

00:17:40.558 --> 00:17:42.194
This was a way for you

00:17:42.227 --> 00:17:44.696
to create your own
music playlist.

00:17:44.729 --> 00:17:47.699
For the first time,
this gave people real control

00:17:47.732 --> 00:17:49.534
over what they were
listening to.

00:17:49.567 --> 00:17:52.437
It wasn't just dictated by
whatever was sold in stores.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:56.208
The mixtape was a really big
part of growing up.

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:57.742
It was a big part
of high school culture.

00:17:57.775 --> 00:17:59.244
Americus:
There was no such thing

00:17:59.277 --> 00:18:01.146
as sending your
Spotify list to your pals.

00:18:01.179 --> 00:18:02.714
You literally were, like,
trading off cassettes.

00:18:02.747 --> 00:18:03.849
"Hey, I made this
cool mixtape."

00:18:03.882 --> 00:18:05.650
"Hey, let me give you mine."

00:18:05.683 --> 00:18:07.152
If you had a crush,
you gave them a mixtape.

00:18:07.185 --> 00:18:08.854
And that was everything
you needed to say

00:18:08.887 --> 00:18:11.190
but were too scared to say.

00:18:12.891 --> 00:18:14.392
But the area where the Walkman

00:18:14.425 --> 00:18:17.195
had the biggest impact
was on exercise.

00:18:17.228 --> 00:18:21.633
Seth: Running, jogging,
aerobics, Jazzercise,

00:18:21.666 --> 00:18:22.901
all these things blowing up

00:18:22.934 --> 00:18:24.569
at the same time
as the Walkman.

00:18:24.602 --> 00:18:26.371
It's like this
perfect marriage.

00:18:26.404 --> 00:18:29.207
Lisa: Sony clearly noticed
that people were using

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.243
the Walkman for exercise,
so they launched

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:33.578
a sport model in the 1980s.

00:18:33.611 --> 00:18:35.413
Americus:
That was actually kind of
genius to be able to say

00:18:35.446 --> 00:18:37.749
this is something that is
a natural product market fit,

00:18:37.782 --> 00:18:40.552
so let's make something specific
called the Sports Walkman

00:18:40.585 --> 00:18:42.888
to be able to tap into that.

00:18:42.921 --> 00:18:46.158
Marques:
The Sony Sports Walkman
was a must-have,

00:18:46.191 --> 00:18:47.759
due to the rugged design
and durability,

00:18:47.792 --> 00:18:49.427
but how does it hold up

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.763
in today's portable
music standards?

00:18:51.796 --> 00:18:53.598
To find out, I'm going to
have to run a stress test

00:18:53.631 --> 00:18:55.600
and see how it functions.

00:18:55.633 --> 00:18:57.769
As some of you may know,
in addition to being
a tech reviewer,

00:18:57.802 --> 00:18:59.671
I'm also a professional
ultimate frisbee player.

00:19:05.777 --> 00:19:08.413
So I do listen to music while
working out all the time.

00:19:08.446 --> 00:19:09.781
This seemed like
a perfect opportunity

00:19:09.814 --> 00:19:12.450
to test the durability
of a Sports Walkman.

00:19:12.483 --> 00:19:14.619 line:0%
To do that,
I brought some friends--

00:19:14.652 --> 00:19:15.620 line:0%
the Gregory Brothers.

00:19:15.653 --> 00:19:17.389 align:start size:25% position:75% line:0%
Welcome.

00:19:17.422 --> 00:19:20.325 line:0%
♪ I'm a fun, I'm a fun,
I'm a fun guy ♪

00:19:20.358 --> 00:19:21.927 line:0%
<i> The Gregory Brothers
 took the internet by storm</i>

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:23.628 line:0%
<i> with their songified versions</i>

00:19:23.661 --> 00:19:25.463 line:0%
<i> of the news and viral videos.</i>

00:19:25.496 --> 00:19:27.199 line:0%
<i> They're savvy musicians,</i>

00:19:27.232 --> 00:19:28.600 line:0%
<i> and they know how
 to throw a frisbee.</i>

00:19:28.633 --> 00:19:30.702
♪ Don't believe me,
just watch ♪

00:19:30.735 --> 00:19:33.471 align:start size:78% position:22%
Thanks for being here.
You guys have also played
a little ultimate.

00:19:33.504 --> 00:19:35.307 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- It's true. Not quite
  at your level.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:36.975 align:start size:88% position:13%
What's been your guys'
experience with the Walkman?

00:19:37.008 --> 00:19:38.743 size:84% position:42%
I had the original Walkman.

00:19:38.776 --> 00:19:40.712 align:start position:0%
The Walkman, for me,
it conjures so many memories.

00:19:40.745 --> 00:19:42.414 align:start position:0%
That was my first
experience with headphones.

00:19:42.447 --> 00:19:44.216 align:start size:69% position:31%
What do you think
of this bright yellow?

00:19:44.249 --> 00:19:45.951 align:start size:78% position:22%
I like it.
If I'm holding it
and exercising,

00:19:45.984 --> 00:19:47.919
a car will not run over me.

00:19:47.952 --> 00:19:51.656 align:start size:72% position:28%
So, ideally we can test
wearability,

00:19:51.689 --> 00:19:53.625 align:start size:75% position:25%
playability, durability.

00:19:53.658 --> 00:19:55.327
Do they play without skipping

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.629 align:start size:59% position:41%
or start to break
or pieces fall off?

00:19:57.662 --> 00:19:59.931 align:start position:0%
You're in luck, because
we have written you a song.

00:19:59.964 --> 00:20:01.233 align:start position:0%
We tried to write
a pump up track

00:20:01.266 --> 00:20:02.968 align:start position:0%
and just dubbed it
onto a cassette,

00:20:03.001 --> 00:20:05.003 align:start size:78% position:22%
- and it's called
  "I'm On Fire."
- All right.

00:20:05.036 --> 00:20:07.672
Marques:
<i> We've recorded the audio
 so you guys can hear</i>

00:20:07.705 --> 00:20:09.674
<i> what I'm hearing
 through my headphones.</i>

00:20:09.707 --> 00:20:13.345 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
Here we go.
I'm getting really
pumped by this song.

00:20:13.378 --> 00:20:14.980 line:0%
<i> ( upbeat dance music
 playing )</i>

00:20:15.013 --> 00:20:16.848
<i> ♪ I'm on fire ♪</i>

00:20:19.751 --> 00:20:21.954
<i> ♪ I'm on fire ♪</i>

00:20:24.389 --> 00:20:26.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
-<i> ♪ I'm on fire ♪ </i>
- This is really heavy.

00:20:26.424 --> 00:20:28.360
I used to think working out
with an iPod touch

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:29.995
on your arm
was huge and clunky.

00:20:30.028 --> 00:20:32.364
This one has a built-in clip.

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:33.865
We're going to have to get you
some more air time, I think,

00:20:33.898 --> 00:20:35.634 align:start position:0%
if we want to jostle
this thing enough.

00:20:35.667 --> 00:20:37.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah.
